<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">'O sole mio</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1898">1898</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;'O sole mio&quot; is a Neapolitan song written in 1898. Its words, in the Neapolitan language, are by Giovanni Capurro; the music is by Eduardo di Capua (1865–1917) and Alfredo Mazzucchi (1878–1972). The title means &quot;my sun&quot; or &quot;my sunshine&quot;. For nearly 75 years after it was published, the music was credited to di Capua alone.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to the traditional account, Eduardo di Capua composed the music in April 1898 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odessa">Odessa</a>, while touring with his father's band. It later emerged that the melody was an elaboration of one of 23 melodies he had bought from Alfredo Mazzucchi in the preceding year. For nearly 75 years after publication the music was generally attributed to di Capua alone. The words, in Neapolitan, are by Giovanni Capurro, and the title translates literally as my sun or my sunshine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In November 1972, shortly after her father's death, Mazzucchi's daughter lodged a declaration with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s Office of Literary, Artistic and Scientific Property. It sought to have him recognised as co-composer of 18 of di Capua's songs, including this one. In October 2002 Maria Alvau, a judge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, upheld the declaration. She ruled that Mazzucchi was a legitimate co-composer, because the songs included melodies he had composed and sold to di Capua in June 1897 with written authorisation to use them freely. As a result the song had not yet entered the public domain in any country that was a party to the Berne Convention during the relevant period, as it would have done in Italy had di Capua been the sole composer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The song has been translated into other languages. Charles W. Harrison recorded the first English version in 1915, and in 1921 William E. Booth-Clibborn wrote the hymn Down from His Glory to the same music. In 1949 Tony Martin recorded There's No Tomorrow, with new words, to the melody; Elvis Presley heard it while stationed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> with the U.S. Army, and after his discharge asked for new lyrics, which became It's Now or Never.</p><p>Many singers have recorded the original, among them Enrico Caruso, Beniamino Gigli, Mario Lanza and Luciano Pavarotti. At the opening ceremony of the 1920 Summer Olympics in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> the song was played in place of the Italian national anthem, whose sheet music had not been delivered to the band. In the 1980s a television advertisement for Cornetto ice cream, broadcast in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, set its Just One Cornetto jingle to the melody.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “'O sole mio” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>'O sole mio</b>&quot; is a Neapolitan song written in 1898.</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-sole-mio">'O sole mio</a></li><li><blockquote>It became one of the most popular songs of the Neapolitan tradition, beside &quot;<b>'O sole mio</b>&quot;, &quot;Funiculì, Funiculà&quot; and &quot;Santa Lucia&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/torna-a-surriento">Torna a Surriento</a></li></ul></section>
